<Project>

  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>

    <!--
      CA1707 bans underscores in member names, and every test method here is named as a sentence:
      `A_file_readable_beyond_its_owner_is_refused`. That is not a style preference - a failing test
      prints its method name and nothing else, so the name is the whole of what a reader gets when
      the build goes red at three in the morning. Renaming these to PascalCase would trade a
      sentence for a run-on word to satisfy a rule written for a public API surface, and none of
      these are public API: this whole directory is IsPackable=false.

      Scoped to tests/ and nowhere else, so the rule still holds over everything that ships.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>

    <!--
      No XML documentation file, so CS1591 stops demanding a <summary> on every [Fact]. The
      sentence-shaped method name is the documentation, and a doc comment restating it is a second
      copy to drift. Where a test needs to say *why* the rule exists rather than what it checks,
      that goes in a comment beside the assertion, which is where somebody looking at a failure is
      already reading.

      The ships-to-users half of the tree keeps GenerateDocumentationFile on.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
